import Applied.Statistics.LehmannScheffe

/-!
# Lehmann–Scheffé theorem

- id: stat-lehmann-scheffe
- area: statistics
- status: formalized
- source: E. L. Lehmann, H. Scheffé, "Completeness, similar regions, and unbiased estimation. Part I", Sankhyā 10 (1950) (theorem number to be confirmed)
- used-in: uniformly minimum-variance unbiased estimation (for example in exponential families)
- prerequisites: conditional expectation and law of total variance (Mathlib); Rao–Blackwell (`Applied/Statistics/RaoBlackwell.lean`)
- formalized-elsewhere: atlas-lean `MathlibExt/Probability/Statistics/Sufficiency/UMVU.lean` :: `umvu_of_complete_sufficient` (assumes completeness and sufficiency of a statistic; the statement here replaces sufficiency by its consequence `HasCommonCondExp`, and no bridge from sufficiency is proved); lean-cksd `ae_eq_of_isCompleteStatistic_of_isUnbiasedEstimator`. Statements read and compared, not compiled here.
- statement-review: unreviewed

Informal statement. Let `P θ` (`θ ∈ Θ`) be probability measures on a measurable space and `m`
a sub-σ-algebra. Assume `m` is complete: every `m`-measurable real function that is integrable
with mean zero under every `P θ` is zero `P θ`-almost surely for every `θ`. Let `U` be
`m`-measurable, integrable under every `P θ`, with `E_θ U = τ θ` for all `θ`. Let `V` be square
integrable under every `P θ` with `E_θ V = τ θ` for all `θ`, and assume there is one
`m`-measurable function `W` that is a version of `E_θ[V | m]` for every `θ`. Then
`Var_θ U ≤ Var_θ V` for every `θ`; in fact `U = E_θ[V | m]` almost surely.

Also proved: under completeness, two `m`-measurable integrable estimators with the same mean
under every `P θ` agree `P θ`-almost surely for every `θ`.

Differences from textbook statements. The statistic `T` is replaced by a sub-σ-algebra `m`
(for `m` generated by `T`, `m`-measurable means "a function of `T`"). Sufficiency is not
assumed as a property of `m`; the only consequence of sufficiency that the proof uses is
assumed for the competitor `V`: its conditional expectation given `m` has a parameter-free
version. Completeness is required of integrable mean-zero functions, as usual. `U` is only
assumed integrable; if `Var_θ V` is finite the conclusion shows `U` is square integrable up to
almost-sure equality.

Not covered: sufficiency as a definition for general models and the fact that it yields a
parameter-free conditional expectation; completeness of specific families (for example
exponential families); bounded completeness; Basu's theorem.
-/

namespace Targets.Statistics

alias lehmannScheffe_variance_le := Applied.Statistics.variance_le_of_isCompleteSubalgebra
alias lehmannScheffe_ae_eq_condExp := Applied.Statistics.ae_eq_condExp_of_isCompleteSubalgebra
alias lehmannScheffe_unique := Applied.Statistics.ae_eq_of_unbiased_of_isCompleteSubalgebra

end Targets.Statistics
